\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The limit covariance is an averaged Green function}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Set #{m=\sqrt{\mu}}. For #{|u|\le 1} define
##{H(u)=\frac{\cosh\bigl(m(1/2-|u|)\bigr)}{2m^3\sinh(m/2)}
+\frac{|u|}{2m^2}.}
Let #{I} be the half-open block from #{\alpha} to #{\beta},
and let #{J} be the half-open block from #{\gamma} to
#{\delta}. Both have length #{1/B} and lie in the unit
interval. The limit
covariance of \ref{fdip-00BO} equals
##{B^2\Bigl(H(\beta-\gamma)+H(\alpha-\delta)
-H(\beta-\delta)-H(\alpha-\gamma)\Bigr).}
The same number is the covariance of the means
#{B\int_I f} and #{B\int_J f} for a field with the covariance
#{G} of \ref{fdip-00BG}.}

\proof{
\p{For #{0<u\le 1} the derivative of #{H} is
##{H'(u)=-\frac{\sinh\bigl(m(1/2-u)\bigr)}{2m^2\sinh(m/2)}
+\frac{1}{2m^2}.}
Thus #{H'(0+)=0}. Evenness gives #{H'(0-)=0}, so #{H'} is
continuous at #{0}. A further derivative off #{0} equals
#{G(u)=\cosh(m(1/2-|u|))/(2m\sinh(m/2))}, which is the
function in \ref{fdip-00BG}. The jump in the first derivative
cancels, so there is no atom in the second derivative.}
\p{On the unit interval the same formula for #{G} has Fourier
coefficients #{1/(\mu+(2\pi p)^2)}. Indeed, for each integer
#{p},
##{\int_0^1 e^{m(1/2-x)}e^{-2\pi i px}\,dx
=\frac{2\sinh(m/2)}{m+2\pi i p},}
and the integral with #{e^{-m(1/2-x)}} replaces the
denominator by #{m-2\pi i p}. Averaging these identities and
dividing by #{2m\sinh(m/2)} leaves
#{1/(\mu+(2\pi p)^2)}. The constant term is #{1/\mu}.}
\p{Integrate the series over a block. For #{p\neq 0} the
integral of #{e^{2\pi i px}} over an interval of length
#{1/B} has square #{sin^2(\pi p/B)/(\pi p)^2}, and the
constant mode contributes #{1/B}. Multiplying by #{B} for
each mean produces the coefficients #{\alpha_p} of
\ref{fdip-00BO}. Termwise integration is legitimate because
#{|\alpha_p|} is bounded by a multiple of #{1/p^2}.}
\p{The same double integral equals the combination of #{H}.
For fixed #{x}, the inner integral of #{G(x-y)} from
#{y=\gamma} to #{y=\delta} equals
#{H'(x-\gamma)-H'(x-\delta)}, since #{H''=G} off #{0} and
#{H'} is continuous. Integrating in #{x} from #{\alpha} to
#{\beta} replaces each #{H'} by #{H}. The endpoint
differences of two blocks inside the unit interval run from
#{-1} to #{1}, endpoints included, where this
antiderivative is the function #{H} above. Multiplying by
#{B^2} is the covariance of the two normalized means.}
}
